close() of Cosmopolitan on Windows (libc/calls/close-nt.c, C64 of
docs/UPSTREAM.md): no FlushFileBuffers() for a file that was open for
writing. The flush made each close of a written file wait for the disk
(about 9 ms on the Windows ARM64 runner of CI, 0.25 ms on the x86_64
runner), and __wrap_close() of c_src/cosmo/beam_com.c holds the lock of
the fd table during the close, so select() of the poll thread waited
too: a round trip over loopback took up to 70 ms while another process
wrote files. Linux, and native Windows programs such as Erlang/OTP for
Windows, do not flush at close; fsync() still flushes.

scripts/steps.sh builds the file with this patch (build_cosmo_file). The
file has no #ifdef __x86_64__, so the object has both halves.

diff --git a/libc/calls/close-nt.c b/libc/calls/close-nt.c
index 1655758..cfc9d48 100644
--- a/libc/calls/close-nt.c
+++ b/libc/calls/close-nt.c
@@ -44,13 +44,12 @@ textwindows int sys_close_nt(int fd, int fildes) {
       if (_weaken(sys_fcntl_nt_lock_cleanup)) {
         _weaken(sys_fcntl_nt_lock_cleanup)(fildes);
       }
-      if ((f->flags & O_ACCMODE) != O_RDONLY &&
-          GetFileType(f->handle) == kNtFileTypeDisk) {
-        // Like Linux, closing a file on Windows doesn't guarantee it is
-        // immediately synced to disk. But unlike Linux this could cause
-        // subsequent operations, e.g. unlink() to break w/ access error
-        FlushFileBuffers(f->handle);
-      }
+      // BEAM.com: no FlushFileBuffers() here. As on Linux, and as in the
+      // close of native Windows programs (Erlang/OTP for Windows too),
+      // close() does not write the data to the disk; fsync() does. The
+      // flush made each close of a written file wait for the disk (about
+      // 9 ms on a runner of CI), and the caller of close() held the lock
+      // of the fd table, so the poll thread waited too (C64).
       break;
     case kFdSocket:
       if (_weaken(sys_closesocket_nt)) {
